We have derived improved differentiability properties for solutions to a non-autonomous obstacle problem, formulated as
  \begin{equation*}
  \min \left\{\int_{\Omega}f\left(x, Dv(x)\right)dx\,:\, v\in
  \mathcal{K}_\psi(\Omega)\right\},
  \end{equation*}
  where the set of admissible functions $\mathcal{K}_\psi(\Omega)$ is defined by
  \begin{equation*}
  \mathcal{K}_\psi(\Omega)=\left\{v\in W^{1, p}(\Omega)\,:\, v\geq \min\{\psi, \tilde{\psi}\}\text{ a.e. on }\Omega\right\}.
  \end{equation*}
  The function $f$ is assumed to exhibit quadratic growth behavior. The problem involves a Obstacle condition. Notably, under the assumption that the boundary of the set of obstacles is regular, we have proven that the gradient of the solution has the same regularity as the obstacles. Also, if one obstacle is superlinear and the other is sublinear, we have shown analogous results.

We investigate a general matrix optimization problem subject to a fixed-rank positive semi-definite constraint and perform the Burer-Monteiro factorization to analyze its landscape under a particular Riemannian perspective. Assuming standard strong convexity and smoothness properties for the objective function $f$, we establish the existence of globally stable parameters that facilitate local algorithms to locally solve the problem. Specifically, we establish the following geodesic convexity properties under different assumptions for $f$:
1) If $f$ is strongly convex and smooth, the transformed problem features a constant geodesic diameter and smoothness, leading to geodesic strong convexity;
2) If $f$ is smooth but not strongly convex, the transformed problem exhibits a smooth but not strongly geodesic convex landscape;
3) If $f$ has a certain sum-of-squares structure, the transformed problem also has a smooth but not strongly geodesic convex landscape.
In contrast to existing landscape analyses, our approach provides a framework in which all stationary points of the factorized problem correspond to global minima, thereby eliminating spurious saddle points. This analysis paves the way for the development of local algorithms capable of efficiently solving a broad spectrum of nonconvex matrix factorization problems.
